{"created": "2025-06-02T19:21:19.739631+00:00", "modified": "2025-06-02T19:21:19.885957+00:00", "id": 15576712, "conceptrecid": "15576711", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.15576712", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.15576711", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15576712", "metadata": {"title": "Non-Perturbative Trivializing Flows for Lattice Gauge Theories", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.15576712", "publication_date": "2025-06-02", "description": "<p>Data generated to produce figures and tables.</p>\n<p>Abstract:</p>\n<div>\n<div>Continuous normalizing flows are known to be highly expressive and flexible, which allows for easier incorporation of large symmetries and makes them a powerful computational tool for lattice field theories. Building on previous work, we present a general continuous normalizing flow architecture for matrix Lie groups that is equivariant under group transformations. We apply this to lattice gauge theories in two dimensions as a proof-of-principle and demonstrate competitive performance, showing its potential as a tool for future lattice computations.</div>\n</div>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Gerdes, Mathis", "affiliation": "University of Amsterdam", "orcid": "0000-0002-2369-1436"}, {"name": "de Haan, Pim", "affiliation": "CuspAI"}, {"name": "Bondesan, Roberto", "affiliation": "Imperial College London"}, {"name": "Cheng, Miranda C. N.", "affiliation": "University of Amsterdam", "orcid": "0000-0002-4651-095X"}], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "arXiv:2410.13161", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "dataset", "scheme": "arxiv"}], "language": "eng", "custom": {"code:programmingLanguage": [{"id": "python", "title": {"en": "Python"}}]}, "resource_type": {"title": "Dataset", "type": "dataset"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "15576711"}}]}}, "title": "Non-Perturbative Trivializing Flows for Lattice Gauge Theories", "lin